    "summary": "Rare Cowboy Song jam, fiery It's A Man's World & intense The Other One",
    "blurb": "The set showcases a rare Cowboy Song jam, a scorching rendition of It’s A Man’s World, and an electrifying The Other One. Jerry steers the acoustic numbers while the electric half builds tension despite some tape warble. Overall the performance is praised for its energy and unique moments.",
    "review": "April 9, 1970 at the Fillmore West delivered a memorable night that still stands out in the Dead’s early‑electric era. The first half rolls through tight acoustic staples before launching into a seldom‑heard Cowboy Song jam – a wild, Marshall‑Tucker‑style romp that reviewers call “intense” and “fiery.” That momentum carries straight into a scorching It’s A Man’s World and later erupts in an expansive The Other One, where the band stretches out with the kind of improvisational daring that defines their legend. While some tape warble mars portions of the acoustic set on certain audience recordings, the soundboard‑dominant source captures the band’s focus and Jerry’s guiding presence throughout. Even with those minor flaws, the show is consistently described as powerful, energetic, and a unique snapshot of the Dead sharing the stage with Miles Davis’ quintet.",
